// Practice quiz: Arithmetic, comparison and logical operators
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/java-basics/arithmetic-comparison-and-logical-operators

::o1 precedence::What is the value of 8 - 2 * 3 + 1? {
=3#Correct. 2 * 3 is 6 first; then 8 - 6 + 1 goes left to right, giving 3.
~19#Not quite. That works left to right ignoring precedence, (8 - 2) * 3 + 1.
~1#Not quite. That subtracts 6 + 1 from 8; + and - go left to right instead.
~-4#Not quite. Multiplication goes first, and then the remaining steps go left to right.
}

::o1 remainder::What is the value of 23 % 4? {
=3#Correct. 23 is 5 groups of 4 (20) with 3 left over.
~5#Not quite. 5 is the quotient, 23 / 4; % gives what is left over.
~5.75#Not quite. That is 23 / 4.0; % gives the remainder.
~0#Not quite. 4 does not divide 23 evenly.
}

::o1 compound assignment::int score \= 4; score *\= 2 + 1; What is score now? {
=12#Correct. The right side 2 + 1 is worked out first, so score \= score * 3.
~9#Not quite. That treats it as score * 2 + 1; a compound assignment uses the whole right side.
~7#Not quite. *\= multiplies; it does not add.
~3#Not quite. score keeps its old value in the calculation, 4 * 3.
}

::o2 both conditions::Which condition is true only when isMember and hasTicket are both true? {
=isMember && hasTicket#Correct. && is true only when both sides are true.
~isMember || hasTicket#Not quite. || is true when at least one side is true, so a member without a ticket passes.
~!isMember && hasTicket#Not quite. ! flips isMember, so this needs a non-member.
~isMember \=\= !hasTicket#Not quite. This is true when exactly one of them is true.
}

::o2 not and or::int x \= 5; What is the value of !(x \=\= 5) || x !\= 3? {
=true#Correct. !(x \=\= 5) is false, but x !\= 3 is true, and || needs only one true side.
~false#Not quite. The left side is false, but the right side, x !\= 3, is true.
~5#Not quite. Comparisons and logical operators give a boolean, not a number.
~A compile error#Not quite. Every part is a boolean expression, so it compiles.
}

::o3 short-circuit or::int n \= 0; boolean b \= n \=\= 0 || 10 / n \=\= 1; What happens? {
=b is true, and the division never runs#Correct. The left side is true, so || already knows the answer and skips the right side.
~ArithmeticException, because of 10 / 0#Not quite. || stops after a true left side, so 10 / n is never evaluated.
~b is false#Not quite. true || anything is true.
~A compile error#Not quite. Both sides are boolean, so it compiles.
}

::o3 guard order::Which line safely checks that total divided by count equals 50 when count may be 0? {
=count !\= 0 && total / count \=\= 50#Correct. When count is 0 the left side is false and && skips the division.
~total / count \=\= 50 && count !\= 0#Not quite. The division runs first and throws when count is 0.
~total / count \=\= 50 || count \=\= 0#Not quite. The division on the left still runs first.
~count \=\= 0 && total / count \=\= 50#Not quite. When count is 0 this runs the division and throws.
}
